ditor lacks required professional qualifications, which the council is working to address. Some lower-priority audit recommendations have been delayed in implementation.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Internal Audit Progress Report Qtr 1 2026 27","paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s51999/51999.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Burnley council draws down reserves at sharp pace as unidentified savings threaten budget","lead":"Burnley council drew \u00a34.979m from its reserves in the first quarter of 2026/27, reducing its earmarked reserves from \u00a324.339m to \u00a319.360m in three months. The council is drawing on its money set aside for known future needs because it is funding capital schemes\u2014buildings, roads and equipment\u2014through internal borrowing, using its own cash instead of taking bank loans to save on interest. However, the papers show this approach's sustainability \"remains under continual review\". At the same time, the council has identified only \u00a383k of the \u00a3259k in budgeted savings it committed to deliver this year, leaving \u00a3176k unaccounted for. The council's own papers state that current economic pressures \"may threaten the achievement of these targets\". If those savings are not found, the council's forecast break-even position will not materialise, and reserves will deplete faster still.","iso":"2026-09-23","paper_title":"Revenue Monitoring Q1 2627 Revenue Report","paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s51968/51968.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Burnley Council faces \u00a31.7m three-year budget gap with no savings plan to close it","lead":"Burnley Council is forecasting a \u00a3215k overspend in the current financial year, a reversal from a budgeted surplus of \u00a339k, because it has failed to identify \u00a3176k of planned savings in the first quarter. The council budgeted \u00a3259k in cost reductions for 2026/27 but has found only \u00a383k so far. Looking ahead, its medium-term financial plan shows budget gaps totalling \u00a31.842m over the next three years, with approved savings of just \u00a3160k. The remaining \u00a31.682m shortfall has no identified proposals to close it. The forecast overspend is compounded by unresolved pay negotiations\u2014a 1% higher pay award than budgeted would cost an additional \u00a3122k\u2014and a \u00a3600k cut in government funding announced at the final settlement in February, which the council has temporarily offset but fears may not continue next year.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Revenue Monitoring Q1 2627 Revenue Report","paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s51881/51881.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","paper_title":"Internal Audit Progress Report Qtr 1 2026 27 Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s52000/52000.pdf","headline":"Burnley received a 'Reasonable Assurance' overall audit opinion for 2025/26, indicating minor control weaknesses exist across the authority","detail":"The council's overall internal audit opinion for 2025/26 was classified as 'Reasonable Assurance', not the highest level of 'Comprehensive Assurance'. According to the audit framework, Reasonable Assurance means the council has a good system of controls but minor weaknesses in design or consistency of application may put achievement of some objectives at risk.","category":"audit","matters":"The overall audit opinion directly reflects the robustness of the council's control environment, which is material to assessing financial and governance risk","figures":"None provided","quote":"There is a good system of controls. However, there are minor weaknesses in the design or consistency of application that may put the achievement of some objectives at risk in the areas reviewed.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","paper_title":"Internal Audit Progress Report Qtr 1 2026 27 Appendix 1","paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s52000/52000.pdf","headline":"Burnley's acquisition of Charter Walk shopping centre was properly governed and has received comprehensive assurance on control of commercial investment risks","detail":"The council acquired Charter Walk shopping centre in Burnley town centre. Internal audit reviewed the acquisition process against risks the National Audit Office had identified for local authority commercial property investments. The audit found that wide-ranging risks presented by the acquisition were identified, controlled and continue to be monitored; risks were explained to members before the decision was taken. The audit gave comprehensive assurance that controls were sound and consistently applied, with no remedial actions required.","category":"capital","matters":"Commercial property investment represents significant financial exposure to the council and is a key factor in assessing financial risk and governance quality for lenders","figures":"None provided","quote":"The acquisition of Charter Walk presented a series of wide-ranging risks to the Council. These were considered and controlled as part of the process and continue to be monitored.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","paper_title":"Internal Audit Mandate Charter and QAIP 2026 Appendix 2 QAIP","paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s51998/51998.pdf","headline":"The Chief Audit Executive is not yet suitability qualified and experienced as required by Global Internal Audit Standards.","detail":"The council's Annual Review of Effectiveness for 2025/26 found that the internal audit service is generally compliant with Global Internal Audit Standards, but identified one area of partial non-compliance: the Chief Audit Executive role lacks the required qualifications and experience. The council notes that 'qualification is being followed', indicating active work to address the gap.","category":"governance","matters":"The Chief Audit Executive leads the council's internal audit function, a critical governance control that lenders rely on to verify financial and operational controls are working properly. A gap in professional standards for this role represents a governance weakness.","figures":"N/A","quote":"One element is a partial compliance and requires the Chief Audit Executive to be suitability qualified and experienced. Qualification is being followed.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","paper_title":"Internal Audit Progress Report Qtr 1 2026 27","paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s51999/51999.pdf","headline":"Some audit recommendations are delayed in implementation despite high-priority actions remaining on target","detail":"The council implemented 100% of high-priority actions from audit plans against its 100% target. However, the report notes that some recommendations that are not classified as high-priority have been delayed in implementation. This suggests potential capacity or prioritization constraints in remediation, even as urgent findings are being addressed.","category":"audit","matters":"Delays in implementing audit recommendations\u2014even lower-priority ones\u2014can indicate control gaps or implementation capacity issues that affect financial governance and risk management, which lenders assess when evaluating council creditworthiness.","figures":"100% of high-priority actions implemented against 100% target; 9 audit reports produced against an annual target of 22","quote":"The percentage of high-priority actions from audit action plans implemented which was 100% and has a target of 100%. It was noted that some of the recommendations (not high priority) had been delayed.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Full Council","paper_title":"Treasury Mgt Q1 Report 2026-27 Final","paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s51957/51957.pdf","headline":"Council maintains under-borrowed position despite capital budget increase","detail":"Despite the capital budget increasing by \u00a36.2m and borrowing need increasing by \u00a30.8m, the council continues to operate with under-borrowed status, meaning it has not reached its approved borrowing limits. The report confirms existing Treasury Limits remain prudent with no policy changes proposed.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Maintaining an under-borrowed position while increasing capital spending demonstrates financial flexibility to borrow further if needed for capital or liquidity requirements.","figures":"Total borrowing Q1: \u00a362.670m; Capital budget increase: \u00a324.832m \u2192 \u00a331.025m; Borrowing need increase: \u00a38.442m \u2192 \u00a39.261m","quote":"with existing Treasury Limits for borrowing, notably the Operational Boundary and Authorised Limit, still deemed prudent, given the Council maintains an under-borrowed position.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-24"},"name":"Burnley","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-26","since_iso":"2026-08-27","distress":false,"headline":"Burnley received a 'Reasonable Assurance' overall audit opinion for 2025/26, indicating minor control weaknesses exist across the authority","lead":"The overall audit opinion directly reflects the robustness of the council's control environment, which is material to assessing financial and governance risk","also":["118 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Scrutiny Committee on 2026-10-14","n_findings":119,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Internal Audit Progress Report Qtr 1 2026 27 Appendix 1","top_paper_url":"https://burnley.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s52000/52000.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":199,"news_drafts_total":3}}